{"Unconditionally calling `pipeline.fetch_state` when reading `installation_id` from the query string can raise if the state isn't set - should retrieve query param first and only call fetch_state when missing": 0.4202212691307068, "Direct indexing into `integration.metadata[\"sender\"][\"login\"]` risks a runtime error if metadata is missing or malformed - should use safer lookup like `integration.metadata.get(\"sender\", {}).get(\"login\")`": 0.9050372838973999, "Catching bare `Exception` when parsing the OAuth token response may hide unexpected failures - should narrow the except to specific parsing/IO errors or at least log unexpected exceptions": 0.5519607663154602, "Inconsistent capitalization of \"GitHub\" in the short error message - `error_short` uses \"Github\" but other messages use \"GitHub\"": 0.0716480165719986, "Hard-coding the `responses.calls` index (responses.calls[2]) is brittle with the new OAuth requests - should iterate over responses.calls and select by URL/method instead": 0.21957151591777802, "In `GitHubInstallation.dispatch`, the new `Integration.objects.get(external_id=installation_id, status=ObjectStatus.ACTIVE)` block will cause fresh installations (where the webhook hasn't yet created the Integration row) to be treated as an invalid installation request - should fall back to `pipeline.next_step()` when integration does not yet exist": 0.5478640198707581, "The new `error()` helper returns a generic 'Invalid installation request.' message for several distinct failure modes - should differentiate error_short/error_long messages to make troubleshooting easier": 0.21618101000785828, "The new helper named `error` is very generic and shadows Python's built-in idiom - should rename to something domain-specific like `render_installation_error`": 0.0034081600606441498, "The OAuth token exchange and parsing logic in `OAuthLoginView.dispatch` largely duplicates what exists in `OAuth2CallbackView` - should refactor into a shared helper": 0.03983507305383682}